+# yvUSDC — Aerodrome USDC/WETH yield vault (Base)
+
+An ERC-4626 vault denominated in USDC. Deposits are paired with WETH into the Aerodrome
+volatile USDC/WETH pool, the LP is staked in that pool's gauge, and a keeper calls `harvest()`
+to claim AERO emissions, sell them for USDC, and compound them back into the position.
+
+> **Read "Risks" before depositing.** This vault holds an LP position, not USDC. A depositor's
+> USDC balance is exposed to ETH price movement and impermanent loss. It is not principal-protected.
+
+```
+                   deposit USDC                          harvest()
+   depositor  ──────────────────►  YieldVault  ◄────────────────────  keeper
+                  yvUSDC shares    (ERC-4626)
+                                        │  idle buffer (5%) stays here
+                                        │  everything above it is pushed to
+                                        ▼
+                        AerodromeUsdcWethStrategy
+                                        │
+              ┌─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┐
+              ▼                         ▼                          ▼
+     Aerodrome Router          Aerodrome vAMM LP           Aerodrome Gauge
+    (swap / add / remove)      (USDC + WETH)              (stakes LP, pays AERO)
+              ▲                         ▲                          │
+              └───────────── AERO sold back to USDC ───────────────┘
+
+     Chainlink USDC/USD + ETH/USD + AERO/USD price every one of those arrows
+```
+
+## Contracts
+
+| File | Role |
+| --- | --- |
+| `src/YieldVault.sol` | ERC-4626 vault. Share accounting, fees, deposit cap, pause, keeper entry point. |
+| `src/strategies/AerodromeUsdcWethStrategy.sol` | Owns the LP position: invest, harvest, unwind. |
+| `src/libraries/FairLpMath.sol` | Manipulation-resistant LP valuation (fair-reserves method). |
+| `src/libraries/ChainlinkLib.sol` | Chainlink reads with staleness / sanity checks. |
+| `src/BaseAddresses.sol` | Base mainnet addresses, each asserted against a live fork in the tests. |
+| `script/Deploy.s.sol` | Deployment, deriving the pool and gauge on-chain. |
+
+The vault talks to the strategy through `IStrategy`, so a replacement strategy can be swapped in
+with `setStrategy` without redeploying the vault or migrating depositors.
+
+## Why these integrations
+
+**Aerodrome, for the DEX.** It is the dominant AMM on Base and the venue where Base's liquidity
+incentives actually land. Two properties made it the right fit rather than just the biggest name:
+
+- *The yield has a real source.* The gauge pays AERO emissions voted to this pool, on top of the
+  0.30% swap fees that accrue into the reserves. `harvest()` has something concrete to claim and
+  compound. A plain Uniswap V2-style pool with no gauge would leave nothing for a keeper to do.
+- *The LP is a fungible ERC-20 over a constant-product curve.* `xy = k` means the position can be
+  valued from reserves with a closed-form, manipulation-resistant formula (below), sized with simple
+  arithmetic, and unwound pro-rata. Uniswap V3 / Aerodrome Slipstream would give far better capital
+  efficiency, but each position is an NFT with a tick range that has to be actively rebalanced, and
+  valuing it correctly is materially harder. That is the wrong trade for a first version — see
+  "What a v2 should change".
+
+**The gauge, not just the pool.** The strategy takes the *gauge* address in its constructor and reads
+the pool back out of `gauge.stakingToken()`. This makes it impossible to stake LP into a gauge that
+pays emissions for a different pool. The constructor additionally checks the reward token is AERO,
+that the pool is volatile rather than stable (the valuation math assumes `xy = k`), and that the
+router derives the same pool address from `(USDC, WETH, volatile)`.
+
+**Chainlink, for pricing.** This is the load-bearing choice. Everything the strategy does is bounded
+by an oracle price, never by the pool's own quote:
+
+| Operation | Guard |
+| --- | --- |
+| `totalAssets()` | LP valued from `k` and oracle prices, not from spot reserves |
+| every swap | `amountOutMin` derived from the oracle, less `maxSlippageBps` |
+| `addLiquidity` | LP received must be worth ≥ the tokens spent, less `maxSlippageBps` |
+| `removeLiquidity` | tokens received must be worth ≥ the LP burned, less `maxSlippageBps` |
+
+Chainlink is used rather than Aerodrome's built-in TWAP because the vault's share price has to be
+correct *in the same transaction* as a deposit or a redemption. A TWAP over recent observations can
+still be pushed by a determined attacker across a few blocks, and on Base a single sequencer orders
+those blocks. The Chainlink feeds used are all first-party Base mainnet feeds, checked for a positive
+answer and a timestamp inside a configured heartbeat; a stale feed halts trading and quoting rather
+than falling back to the pool.
+
+### Fair-reserves LP pricing
+
+Pricing an LP token as `reserve0 * price0 + reserve1 * price1` is the classic way to lose a vault.
+An attacker flash-borrows, pushes the pool far from market, mints or burns shares at the distorted
+price, restores the pool, and keeps the difference.
+
+`FairLpMath` instead reconstructs the reserves the pool *would* hold if it were balanced at the
+oracle prices. For a constant-product pool, `k = r0 · r1` is invariant under swaps, so:
+
+```
+balanced at oracle prices:  r0' · p0 == r1' · p1   and   r0' · r1' == k
+  =>  poolValue = r0'·p0 + r1'·p1 = 2 · √k · √(p0 · p1)
+```
+
+The result depends only on `k` and the oracle prices. Trading against the pool cannot move it —
+`test_valuationIsInvariantToReserveManipulation` moves the spot price 16× and the valuation does not
+budge, while a naive spot valuation inflates by more than 50%. Fees earned by the pool *do* raise
+`k`, so genuine LP appreciation is picked up automatically, harvest or no harvest.
+
+## Deployment
+
+Prerequisites: `foundry`, a Base RPC, and a funded deployer key.
+
+```bash
+forge install
+forge build
+forge test
+```
+
+Set the environment, then run the script:
+
+```bash
+export PRIVATE_KEY=0x...          # deployer
+export OWNER=0x...                # multisig or timelock — owns vault + strategy
+export TREASURY=0x...             # receives performance-fee shares
+export KEEPER=0x...               # allowed to call vault.harvest()
+export DEPOSIT_CAP=250000000000   # optional, USDC units (default 250,000 USDC)
+export BASE_RPC_URL=https://...
+export BASESCAN_API_KEY=...
+
+forge script script/Deploy.s.sol:Deploy --rpc-url base --broadcast --verify
+```
+
+The script resolves the USDC/WETH pool from `Router.poolFor(...)` and its gauge from
+`Voter.gauges(pool)`, and refuses to continue if the gauge is missing, dead, or points at a different
+pool. No pool or gauge address is hardcoded anywhere.
+
+It deploys the vault owned by the deployer so it can wire everything in one transaction, then calls
+`transferOwnership(OWNER)`. **Ownership is two-step: `OWNER` must call `acceptOwnership()` on both
+the vault and the strategy before the deployment is complete.** Verify afterwards:
+
+```bash
+cast call $VAULT "owner()(address)"     --rpc-url base   # == OWNER
+cast call $STRATEGY "owner()(address)"  --rpc-url base   # == OWNER
+cast call $VAULT "strategy()(address)"  --rpc-url base   # == STRATEGY
+cast call $VAULT "totalAssets()(uint256)" --rpc-url base
+```
+
+Start with a low `DEPOSIT_CAP` and raise it as the position proves out. The cap should be sized
+against the pool, not against demand — see "Operational limits".
+
+### Base addresses used
+
+| | Address |
+| --- | --- |
+| USDC | `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` |
+| WETH | `0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000006` |
+| AERO | `0x940181a94A35A4569E4529A3CDfB74e38FD98631` |
+| Aerodrome Router | `0xcF77a3Ba9A5CA399B7c97c74d54e5b1Beb874E43` |
+| Aerodrome PoolFactory | `0x420DD381b31aEf6683db6B902084cB0FFECe40Da` |
+| Aerodrome Voter | `0x16613524e02ad97eDfeF371bC883F2F5d6C480A5` |
+| Chainlink USDC/USD | `0x7e860098F58bBFC8648a4311b374B1D669a2bc6B` |
+| Chainlink ETH/USD | `0x71041dddad3595F9CEd3DcCFBe3D1F4b0a16Bb70` |
+| Chainlink AERO/USD | `0x4EC5970fC728C5f65ba413992CD5fF6FD70fcfF0` |
+
+Every one of these is asserted against live Base state by `test/fork/BaseFork.t.sol`, including
+symbols, decimals, feed decimals, and that the gauge is alive.
+
+## Keeper operation
+
+One entry point:
+
+```solidity
+vault.harvest() returns (uint256 rewardsUsdc, uint256 feeShares, uint256 deployed)
+```
+
+Restricted to addresses in `isKeeper` (and the owner). In order, it:
+
+1. pushes idle USDC above the 5% liquidity buffer into the strategy;
+2. claims AERO from the gauge and sells it for USDC along `rewardRoute`;
+3. mints `performanceFeeBps` of the realised rewards to the treasury as shares;
+4. swaps half the strategy's idle USDC for WETH, adds liquidity, and stakes the LP.
+
+Deposits and the harvest are deliberately deployed in the same pass, so one harvest makes one swap
+rather than two.
+
+**Cadence.** Once or twice a day is the right starting point. There is no time-based state, so
+harvesting more often is safe but wasteful: each pass pays gas and one round of swap fees, and the
+depth cap (below) means it may not deploy much more than a less frequent pass would. Harvesting less
+often is also safe — AERO keeps accruing in the gauge and `pendingRewards()` reports it.
+
+**Suggested trigger.** Call when `strategy.pendingRewards()` exceeds a value that comfortably covers
+gas, or when `vault.idleAssets()` has drifted well above the buffer. Both are plain view calls.
+
+**Simulate first.** `harvest()` reverts rather than trading at a bad price. Always `eth_call` it
+before broadcasting, and skip the block on revert instead of retrying blindly:
+
+| Revert | Meaning | Action |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `StalePrice(feed, updatedAt)` | a Chainlink feed is past its heartbeat | wait; alert if it persists |
+| `SlippageExceeded(out, minOut)` | the pool disagrees with the oracle by more than `maxSlippageBps` | wait for arbitrage to realign the pool |
+| `INSUFFICIENT_OUTPUT_AMOUNT` | same, caught by the router's own check | as above |
+| `NotKeeper(caller)` | keeper not authorised | check `isKeeper` |
+
+None of these strand funds. Emissions keep accruing in the gauge and the position stays staked.
+
+**Public view calls worth monitoring:** `vault.totalAssets()`, `vault.convertToAssets(1e18)` (share
+price — should only fall on a harvest that deploys new capital, see "Costs"), `vault.idleAssets()`,
+`strategy.pendingRewards()`, `strategy.totalLpBalance()`.
+
+### Owner runbook
+
+| Task | Call |
+| --- | --- |
+| Stop new deposits | `vault.pause()` |
+| Stop compounding, keep claiming | `strategy.setEmergencyExit(true)` |
+| Unwind the position in stages | `strategy.unwind(type(uint256).max)`, repeatedly |
+| Retire / replace the strategy | `vault.setStrategy(newOrZero)` |
+| Retune trade sizing | `strategy.setMaxTradeReserve(bps)`, `setMaxSlippage(bps)` |
+| Repoint the AERO sale | `strategy.setRewardRoute(route)` |
+
+**Migration order matters.** `setStrategy` calls `liquidateAll()` and reverts if more than a dust
+amount is left behind, and one transaction can only unwind a bounded slice of the pool. So:
+run a final `harvest()` while the strategy is still attached, then `unwind(type(uint256).max)`
+repeatedly across separate blocks until `totalLpBalance()` is zero and no WETH is left, and only
+then call `setStrategy`.
+
+## Fees and costs
+
+| Parameter | Default | Cap | Goes to |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `performanceFeeBps` | 10% | 20% | treasury, as shares, on realised rewards only |
+| `withdrawalFeeBps` | 0.50% | 1% | **stays in the vault** |
+| `bufferBps` | 5% | — | idle USDC held for small exits |
+
+The withdrawal fee is not revenue. Unwinding an LP position costs real slippage, and someone has to
+pay it. Rather than silently returning less than `previewRedeem` promised — which would break
+ERC-4626 — the vault quotes the fee up front and keeps it, so the cost lands on the exiting holder
+instead of being socialised to everyone who stayed. Only half of an unwind has to be swapped, so
+`withdrawalFeeBps ≥ maxSlippageBps / 2` is what makes an exit self-funding, including the final exit
+where there is no remaining position to over-unwind against. Keep that relationship if you retune.
+
+**Entry cost is socialised.** The first harvest that deploys new capital swaps half of it, which
+costs the pool fee plus price impact — roughly 0.2% at default settings. It shows up as a one-off dip
+in the share price, borne by all holders at deploy time, and is recovered by emissions.
+`test_sharePriceRisesOnceTheCapitalIsDeployed` pins this behaviour down explicitly.
+
+`previewDeposit` / `previewMint` / `previewWithdraw` / `previewRedeem` are all exact — integration
+tests assert `redeem()` returns precisely what `previewRedeem()` quoted, and that a
+`previewRedeem → previewWithdraw` round trip can never return more shares than it started with.
+
+## Operational limits
+
+**Trade size is capped by pool depth.** On a constant-product pool, swapping a fraction `f` of a
+reserve costs roughly `f + poolFee` against the mid price. `maxTradeReserveBps` (default 0.25%) caps
+every swap at that fraction of the relevant reserve, which is what actually keeps each operation
+inside `maxSlippageBps` (default 1%) given Aerodrome's 0.30% volatile fee. Keep
+`maxTradeReserveBps + poolFee` comfortably under `maxSlippageBps`; the default leaves headroom for
+the pool being somewhat off-oracle when the keeper arrives.
+
+Two consequences, both intentional:
+
+- **Deposits deploy over several harvests.** A large deposit is not rejected; it sits as idle USDC
+  (still counted in `totalAssets`, still withdrawable without slippage) and is deployed a slice at a
+  time. Anything the cap holds back simply waits for the next pass.
+- **Large exits are staged, not blocked.** A redemption bigger than one call can unwind reverts with
+  `InsufficientLiquidity(requested, available)`, naming what is available now. Splitting the exit
+  across transactions clears it at essentially the same total value —
+  `test_oversizedExitRevertsThenClearsInChunks` checks precisely that.
+
+**Size the deposit cap to the pool, not to demand.** At the time of writing, the live
+Aerodrome vAMM USDC/WETH pool holds roughly 1,800 WETH and 4.2M USDC (≈ $8.3M). Against that depth
+the fork tests measure a single-transaction exit capacity in the tens of thousands of USDC. The
+250,000 USDC default cap is about 3% of the pool; going materially above that makes exits slow and
+the position harder to unwind in a stress event.
+
+## Risks
+
+- **This is not a USDC-yield product.** Half the deposit is converted to WETH. If ETH moves, the
+  USDC-denominated value moves with it, minus impermanent loss. A depositor who wanted USDC exposure
+  and gets back less USDC after ETH falls has not been rugged — that is what an LP position does.
+  Anything front-ending this vault must say so plainly.
+- **Round-trip cost.** Deposit → deploy → unwind → withdraw pays swap fees and slippage twice, plus
+  the withdrawal fee. Short holding periods lose money; the position needs time in the gauge.
+- **Oracle risk.** Correctness depends on three Chainlink feeds. A stale feed halts harvests and
+  `totalAssets()` — safe, but it does block deposits and withdrawals until it recovers. A *wrong*
+  feed would mis-price shares; the heartbeats and positive-answer checks are the only defence, and
+  there is no secondary oracle in this version.
+- **Oracle lag.** Feeds update on deviation or heartbeat, so between updates the vault's share price
+  can lag the market slightly. Round-tripping that gap costs the withdrawal fee, which makes it
+  unattractive at realistic sizes, but it is not eliminated.
+- **Emissions risk.** AERO emissions to this pool are set by veAERO voting each epoch and can fall to
+  near zero. The vault keeps working; the yield does not.
+- **Admin trust.** The owner can replace the strategy, retune slippage, and pause deposits. It cannot
+  withdraw user funds directly — `sweep` explicitly rejects USDC, WETH, AERO and the LP token — but a
+  malicious strategy could. Use a multisig, ideally behind a timelock.
+- **Not audited.** This is a first version.
+
+## Testing
+
+```bash
+forge test                                       # 70 local tests, no network needed
+forge test --gas-report
+BASE_RPC_URL=https://mainnet.base.org forge test --match-path 'test/fork/*'   # + 6 fork tests
+```
+
+| Suite | Covers |
+| --- | --- |
+| `test/unit/FairLpMath.t.sol` | valuation invariance under manipulation, fee accrual, fuzzed |
+| `test/unit/ChainlinkLib.t.sol` | staleness, incomplete rounds, future timestamps, bad decimals |
+| `test/unit/YieldVault.t.sol` | ERC-4626 semantics, preview exactness, fees, cap, pause, access control, inflation attack |
+| `test/unit/Strategy.t.sol` | wiring checks, invest/harvest/withdraw, depth caps, oracle guards |
+| `test/integration/Lifecycle.t.sol` | multi-depositor flows, flash-loan sandwich, staged exits, migration |
+| `test/fork/BaseFork.t.sol` | every Base address and ABI, real gauge, real emissions, real round trip |
+
+The local suite runs against mocks that reproduce Aerodrome's actual semantics — fee taken off the
+input, `MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY` lock, optimal-ratio `addLiquidity` — sized to be in the same ballpark as
+the live pool. Both suites model the arbitrage that realigns the pool between keeper passes
+(`test/helpers/PoolArb.sol`); without it, consecutive harvests drift further and further from the
+oracle for reasons that never occur on a live chain.
+
+The fork tests are what verify the integration is real: they stake into the actual gauge, warp three
+days, and observe genuine AERO accrue before compounding it.
+
+## What a v2 should change
+
+- **Aerodrome Slipstream (concentrated liquidity)** for far better capital efficiency, at the cost of
+  NFT position management, tick rebalancing, and harder valuation.
+- **A withdrawal queue** so large exits are serviced by the keeper across blocks instead of being
+  refused, and the depth cap stops being user-visible.
+- **A second oracle** (a bounded Aerodrome TWAP as a sanity check on Chainlink) so a single feed
+  failure degrades rather than halts.
+- **Optional hedging** of the WETH leg, if the product is meant to be USDC-denominated yield rather
+  than an LP position with extra steps.
